#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:142: ../mm/maccess.c:147
msgid "``void __user *dst``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:419
msgid ""
"Destination address, in userspace. This buffer must be **ksize** bytes long."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:421
msgid "(Alleged) size of **dst** struct."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:423
#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:142: ../mm/maccess.c:144
msgid "``const void *src``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:423
msgid "Size of **src** struct."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:425
msgid "``bool *ignored_trailing``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:424
msgid ""
"Set to ``true`` if there was a non-zero byte in **src** that userspace "
"cannot see because they are using an smaller struct."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:426
msgid ""
"Copies a struct from kernel space to userspace, in a way that guarantees "
"backwards-compatibility for struct syscall arguments (as long as future "
"struct extensions are made such that all new fields are *appended* to the "
"old struct, and zeroed-out new fields have the same meaning as the old "
"struct)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:432
msgid ""
"Some syscalls may wish to make sure that userspace knows about everything in "
"the struct, and if there is a non-zero value that userspce doesn't know "
"about, they want to return an error (such as -EMSGSIZE) or have some other "
"fallback (such as adding a \"you're missing some information\" flag). If "
"**ignored_trailing** is non-``NULL``, it will be set to ``true`` if there "
"was a non-zero byte that could not be copied to userspace (ie. was past "
"**usize**)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:439
msgid ""
"While unconditionally returning an error in this case is the simplest "
"solution, for maximum backward compatibility you should try to only return "
"-EMSGSIZE if the user explicitly requested the data that couldn't be copied. "
"Note that structure sizes can change due to header changes and simple "
"recompilations without code changes(!), so if you care about "
"**ignored_trailing** you probably want to make sure that any new field data "
"is associated with a flag. Otherwise you might assume that a program knows "
"about data it does not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:448
msgid ""
"**ksize** is just sizeof(*src), and **usize** should've been passed by "
"userspace. The recommended usage is something like the following::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:476
msgid ""
"If **usize** < **ksize**, then the kernel is trying to pass userspace a "
"newer struct than it supports. Thus we only copy the interoperable portions "
"(**usize**) and ignore the rest (but **ignored_trailing** is set to ``true`` "
"if any of the trailing (**ksize** - **usize**) bytes are non-zero)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:480
msgid ""
"If **usize** > **ksize**, then the kernel is trying to pass userspace an "
"older struct than userspace supports. In order to make sure the unknown-to-"
"the-kernel fields don't contain garbage values, we zero the trailing "
"(**usize** - **ksize**) bytes."
msgstr ""

