msgid ""
"The scope is internally implemented as a autoterminating nested for() loop, "
"which can be left with 'return', 'break' and 'goto' at any point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:786
msgid ""
"When the scope is left user_##**_mode**##_access_end() is automatically "
"invoked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:789
msgid ""
"When the architecture supports masked user access and the access region "
"which is determined by **uptr** and **size** is not a valid user space "
"address, i.e. < TASK_SIZE, the scope sets the pointer to a faulting user "
"space address and does not terminate early. This optimizes for the good case "
"and lets the performance uncritical bad case go through the fault."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:795
msgid ""
"The eventual modification of the pointer is limited to the scope. Outside of "
"the scope the original pointer value is unmodified, so that the original "
"pointer value is available for diagnostic purposes in an out of scope fault "
"path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:800
msgid ""
"Nesting scoped user access into a user access scope is invalid and fails the "
"build. Nesting into other guards, e.g. pagefault is safe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:803
msgid ""
"The masked variant does not check the size of the access and relies on a "
"mapping hole (e.g. guard page) to catch an out of range pointer, the first "
"access to user memory inside the scope has to be within **uptr** ... "
"**uptr** + PAGE_SIZE - 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:11: ../include/linux/uaccess.h:808
