#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:71
msgid "The reference count on this folio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:73
msgid ""
"Folios contain a reference count.  When that reference count reaches zero, "
"the folio is referred to as frozen.  At this point, it will usually be "
"returned to the memory allocator, but some parts of the kernel freeze folios "
"in order to perform unusual operations on them such as splitting or "
"migration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:79
msgid ""
"The refcount is usually incremented by calls to folio_get() and decremented "
"by calls to folio_put().  Some typical users of the folio refcount:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:83
msgid "Each reference from a page table"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:84
msgid "The page cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:85
msgid "Filesystem private data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:86
msgid "The LRU list"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:87
msgid "Pipes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:88
msgid "Direct IO which references this page in the process address space"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:90
msgid ""
"The reference count has three components: expected, temporary and spurious.  "
"The expected reference count of a folio is that which we would logically "
"expect it to be from just reading the code. Temporary refcounts are gained "
"by threads which need a temporary reference to make sure the folio isn't "
"reallocated while they use it. Spurious refcounts are gained by threads "
"which, thanks to RCU walks of the page tables or file cache, find a stale "
"pointer to a folio. These threads will drop the refcount after discoveering "
"the pointer is stale, but it can surprise other users to see the spurious "
"refcount on a freshly allocated folio (eg they may see a refcount of 2 "
"instead of 1)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:103
msgid "The number of references to this folio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:264
msgid "Attempt to increase the refcount on a folio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:266
msgid ""
"If you do not already have a reference to a folio, you can attempt to get "
"one using this function.  It may fail if, for example, the folio has been "
"freed since you found a pointer to it, or it is frozen for the purposes of "
"splitting or migration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../core-api/mm-api:121: ../include/linux/page_ref.h:272
msgid "True if the reference count was successfully incremented."
msgstr ""

